  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is to use the <b>NoEcho</b> property for the parameter in your CloudFormation template. By setting <b>NoEcho</b> to <b>true</b> on the parameter, you ensure that when users describe or view the stack, the actual value (such as your database password) is masked and appears as a sequence of asterisks. This is a best practice for handling confidential data entered via parameters.</p><p>The option <i>Use the password attribute in the definition of the resource</i> is incorrect because CloudFormation resources do not have a \"password\" attribute that controls output visibility. The <i>Set the hidden property for the parameter value</i> and <i>Add the hidden attribute to the resource in CloudFormation</i> options are also incorrect, as neither \"hidden\" property nor \"hidden\" attribute are valid options for masking parameter values or controlling secrets visibility in CloudFormation.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> Always use NoEcho for sensitive information in parameters, but remember that values might still be visible elsewhere (such as in logs or outputs) unless handled properly.</p>",
